      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{3}^{x}+1?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/17a76468bd4f446f420d784ae479724135b2af3369a7d69f87c67507e74a8901.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{3}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/ac5e769c069b753bbf3f6a9a276b69881a3d2699afc4f62498233ecc526f5a2b.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit up to give $y=-{3}^{x}+1.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,0)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/c69c8be5828709cef483cba56d5842b973cf718e7592a8fba9e825be08204f5b.png)",
